description:
Custom types of "kind" slice that satisfy the `unmarshaller` interface run into a problem, because `UnmarshalText` intends to unmarshal just a single entry of the slice. As a result, we must handle these cases directly by splitting the environment variable on `,` and calling `UnmarshalText` on each part.

We add such a test struct into the tests to ensure that this behavior works as intended.

This is an extension of #6 . The way that `gcfg` works, `UnmarshalText` is called _per entry_ in the `gcfg` file. As a result, we should expect `UnmarshalText` to be called on each element of a `encoding.TextUnmarshaler` _after_ splitting it into parts using our `,` convention for slices.

A small modification to our example in #6 illustrates this:

<details>

```
package main

import (
	"gopkg.in/gcfg.v1"
	"os"
	"fmt"
        "strings"
)

// StringSliceType is a slice type to test encoding.TextUnmarshaler behaviour.
type StringSliceType []string

func (ss *StringSliceType) Length() int {
	return ss.Length()
}

func (ss *StringSliceType) Set(text string) error {
	*ss = append(*ss, text)
	return nil
}

func (ss *StringSliceType) UnmarshalText(text []byte) error {
        if string(text) == "two" {
          *ss = append(*ss, "HITWO")
        } else if string(text) == "three" {
          *ss = append(*ss, "HITHREE")
        } else {
	  *ss = append(*ss, string(text))
        }
	return nil
}

func (ss *StringSliceType) String() string {
	return strings.Join(*ss, "|")
}

func check(e error) {
	if e != nil {
		panic(e)
	}
}

type TestConfig struct {
	One StringSliceType
}

type Config struct {
	Test TestConfig
}

func main() {

	cfg := Config{}

	c := []byte("[test]\none=one\none=two\none=three\n")
	err := os.WriteFile("/tmp/test", c, 0644)
	check(err)

	gcfg.ReadFileInto(&cfg, "/tmp/test")
	fmt.Printf("Config Setting: %v\n", cfg.Test.One)
	gcfg.ReadFileInto(&cfg, "/tmp/test")
	fmt.Printf("Config Setting: %v\n", cfg.Test.One)
}
```

Returning:
```
go run .
Config Setting: [one HITWO HITHREE]
Config Setting: [one HITWO HITHREE one HITWO HITHREE]
```

</details>

The `gcfg` package appends arrays, as shown in the below example:

```
package main

import (
	"gopkg.in/gcfg.v1"
	"os"
	"fmt"
)

func check(e error) {
	if e != nil {
		panic(e)
	}
}

type TestConfig struct {
	One []string
}

type Config struct {
	Test TestConfig
}

func main() {

	cfg := Config{}

	c := []byte("[test]\none=two\none=three\n")
	err := os.WriteFile("/tmp/test", c, 0644)
	check(err)

	gcfg.ReadFileInto(&cfg, "/tmp/test")
	fmt.Printf("Config Setting: %v\n", cfg.Test.One)
	gcfg.ReadFileInto(&cfg, "/tmp/test")
	fmt.Printf("Config Setting: %v\n", cfg.Test.One)
}
```

resulting in:
```
Config Setting: [two three]
Config Setting: [two three two three]
```

`gcfgenv`, by contrast, has overwritten arrays to date. This PR adds tests for this behavior and alters `gcfgenv` to be consistent with `gcfg`. However, a noteworthy side effect:
> parsing the same environment variables into the same config object twice will duplicate values in the output
